WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.020
- And as time was up

00:00:02.020 --> 00:00:05.270
and she turned in her
Contracts final exam,

00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:07.500
she realized she was in the wrong class

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:10.770
and she had done the wrong final (gasps).

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:12.670
Today, we're talking about real life

00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:14.447
law school horror stories.

00:00:14.447 --> 00:00:15.430
(imitates ghost)

00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:16.393
Scary.

00:00:17.265 --> 00:00:19.765
(light music)

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:22.720
Hey, legal eagles.

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:25.740
D. James Stone here teaching
you how to think like a lawyer

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:28.190
so you can crush law school.

00:00:28.190 --> 00:00:30.980
Law school can be a really scary place.

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:35.400
It's three years long, and in
that time, a lot can go wrong.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:37.470
So I thought I'd share
some of the scariest things

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:40.310
that actually happened
to me in law school.

00:00:40.310 --> 00:00:43.960
So let's dig in to some
of these scary stories.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:45.550
One of the first things that happens

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:47.320
when you get to law school

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:49.950
is that you are surrounded by
some of the smartest people

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:52.040
you've ever met in your entire life.

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:53.770
I don't really care
which law school it is.

00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:57.140
You're gonna be surrounded
by A type personalities.

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:00.150
And some of those people
are what are called gunners.

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:03.270
Gunners are the people that
always raise their hand,

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:05.080
they always seem to have the answer.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:07.170
And when you first get to law school,

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:09.090
they can seem really intimidating

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:11.360
because people don't like to answer

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:13.020
the professor's questions.

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:16.330
So I had a few gunners in my class.

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:20.070
Hopefully, I was not one of those gunners,

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:22.580
even though I liked to answer on occasion.

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:27.580
But there was one particular
gunner in my class.

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:31.340
For the sake of anonymity,
let's call him Alan.

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:33.970
Alan was hardcore.

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:36.970
He would answer every
professor's question.

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:41.760
He had a background in
mathematics and philosophy.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:46.110
So he was able to talk about
the philosophical underpinnings

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:49.570
of different legal doctrines,

00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:52.400
he was able to think logically,

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:55.700
and he always seemed to have the answer.

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:58.550
So after your first semester,

00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:03.050
you are given a slip of
paper with your grades in it,

00:02:03.050 --> 00:02:07.530
and I happened to be sitting
behind Alan in class,

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:09.570
in between classes when we were getting

00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:11.770
our grades for the first time.

00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:13.410
Basically, you open up a slip of paper

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:15.670
and that's your report card.

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:20.670
And I happened to see that he
got all Cs his first semester.

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:22.000
I was shocked.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:23.810
I had never seen anything like it.

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:28.810
One of, if not the smartest
person in class got all Cs.

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:30.580
Let it be a lesson to you

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:33.840
that, just because someone
is very good in class

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:36.630
and seems to be one of the
smartest people in the room,

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:38.980
it doesn't mean they're gonna
get good grades in law school.

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:42.690
And this person eventually
became a friend of mine,

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:47.690
but man, it was quite a shock
to see that this person,

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:50.740
who seemed to know all the answers,

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:53.140
got straight Cs his first semester.

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:55.520
Another horror story that happened to me

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:59.030
relates to my mock trial competition.

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:00.500
I was on the mock trial team

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:02.600
for all three years in law school.

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:06.410
It was one of the best
experiences that I had.

00:03:06.410 --> 00:03:09.770
Mock trial is where you
pretend to be a lawyer,

00:03:09.770 --> 00:03:12.460
you conduct a trial, and
you are scored by judges

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:14.250
based on how well you do.

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:17.223
It's just like mock trial
in high school and college.

00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:21.390
And part of the mock trial competition

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:23.690
is traveling to different cities.

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:28.000
Well, one of the competitions
was held in Las Vegas.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:32.210
In fact, it was one of the national finals

00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:34.710
for this pariticular
mock trial competition.

00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:39.150
And after my team had done their round

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:41.390
and we had actually conducted a round

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:43.840
in front of a real judge

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:47.770
who actually tried cases in Las Vegas,

00:03:47.770 --> 00:03:50.660
the mock trial judge started to brag

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:53.270
to my team and the other team

00:03:53.270 --> 00:03:55.890
about what happened one night

00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:59.310
when a burglar tried to
break into his house.

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:03.460
So this sitting judge regaled
us for about 15 minutes

00:04:03.460 --> 00:04:07.450
about how an intruder tried
to break into his house

00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:09.720
and what he did with this intruder.

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:13.650
Well apparently, to hear
this judge tell the tale,

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:16.720
he sat the intruder down on a chair,

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:20.730
tied him up, put him in a small bathroom,

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:22.880
and then for the next hour or two,

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:26.060
proceeded to threaten him
with a gun to his head

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:30.380
and told him never to
come back to his house.

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:33.950
I'm guessing that, if
that actually happened,

00:04:33.950 --> 00:04:38.700
that that burglar never
burgled that guy's house again.

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:40.570
And this is, again, a sitting judge.

00:04:40.570 --> 00:04:42.940
I don't know if that's
a true story or not.

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:46.920
This was Vegas, after
all, so I probably believe

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:48.980
that this judge is an insane person

00:04:48.980 --> 00:04:53.520
who threatened a burglar with a firearm.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:55.710
So that was an interesting experience.

00:04:55.710 --> 00:04:59.420
Another horror story that happened to me

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:04.050
was that, when I was sitting
for the California Bar Exam,

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:07.780
it was at a hotel in West L.A.

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:10.580
Not just in the regular part of the hotel,

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:14.450
but three floors down in the basement.

00:05:14.450 --> 00:05:16.210
And this was a huge cavern.

00:05:16.210 --> 00:05:18.680
I mean there must have
been 500 people, at least,

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:20.000
sitting for this exam.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:22.570
And so, on day one of the exam,

00:05:22.570 --> 00:05:27.260
we were into the second essay
portion of the Bar Exam,

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:32.070
and all of a sudden, as
what happens in California,

00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:33.760
everything started to shake.

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:35.100
There was an earthquake.

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:40.063
I later learned that it was
over a 5.0 on the Richter scale.

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:43.000
So it was enough to get the chandeliers

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:45.823
in the basement to swing back and forth.

00:05:46.750 --> 00:05:50.790
And all of the people who
were not native Californians

00:05:50.790 --> 00:05:55.370
hid under their desk and were shaken,

00:05:55.370 --> 00:05:57.163
literally and figuratively,

00:05:58.310 --> 00:06:02.700
and they lost some crucial
time on the Bar Exam.

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:05.130
I and the rest of the native Californians

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:07.340
just simply couldn't be bothered

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:10.440
and carried along writing our essays.

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:12.760
I actually feel that
that gave me an advantage

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:15.770
on the Bar Exam because I think
the people from out of town

00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:18.020
were a little bit frazzled,

00:06:18.020 --> 00:06:20.823
whereas, being a native
Southern Californian,

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:26.198
it was just a normal
Tuesday afternoon for me.

00:06:26.198 --> 00:06:28.690
So I give partial credit to the earthquake

00:06:28.690 --> 00:06:30.890
for helping me pass the
California Bar Exam.

00:06:32.710 --> 00:06:35.210
Another horror story that happened to me

00:06:35.210 --> 00:06:40.210
was that my computer
crashed right before finals.

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:44.630
So this was over 11 or 12 years ago.

00:06:44.630 --> 00:06:46.320
So this was back in the day

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:49.440
when computers didn't have
solid-state hard drives

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:53.520
and Dropbox and Google
Drive did not exist.

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:58.100
So I was relying on the files

00:06:58.100 --> 00:07:01.870
that were physically
residing on my computer

00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:03.820
and my hard drive just stopped working.

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:08.150
There were clear signs that
something was going wrong,

00:07:08.150 --> 00:07:11.660
so I had the wherewithal to transfer

00:07:11.660 --> 00:07:14.870
some of these files to, I don't know,

00:07:14.870 --> 00:07:16.840
a floppy disk or a Zip drive

00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:18.830
or maybe I burned it to a CD.

00:07:18.830 --> 00:07:21.460
I'm not sure, but I was
able to get my notes

00:07:21.460 --> 00:07:24.830
off of the computer
probably about 45 minutes

00:07:24.830 --> 00:07:27.420
before the thing just stopped
and never started up again.

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:29.220
So I got really lucky.

00:07:29.220 --> 00:07:31.570
It still was very frazzling.

00:07:31.570 --> 00:07:35.260
I lost a lot of files just before finals

00:07:35.260 --> 00:07:37.343
in my second year of law school.

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:40.800
But that's why I am very adamant

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:43.850
to current and future law students

00:07:43.850 --> 00:07:48.240
to always use cloud
services, backup your files.

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:51.450
There's a chance that you
will never get them back.

00:07:51.450 --> 00:07:52.560
Laptops fail.

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:54.150
They're stolen all the time.

00:07:54.150 --> 00:07:57.400
You never know when your
computer's going to crash

00:07:57.400 --> 00:08:00.140
and you can bet that it's
probably going to happen

00:08:00.140 --> 00:08:02.280
at the worst possible time.

00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:06.380
So do yourself a favor and
backup your crucial files.

00:08:06.380 --> 00:08:08.530
And my final law school horror story

00:08:08.530 --> 00:08:11.440
is that, after three years of law school,

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:16.440
over six semesters, I graduated
with over $160,000 in debt.

00:08:19.940 --> 00:08:21.140
Yeah.

00:08:21.140 --> 00:08:23.680
That's more than a lot
of people's houses cost.

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:27.350
That's three BMWs' worth of debt.

00:08:27.350 --> 00:08:29.370
And that was over 10 years ago.

00:08:29.370 --> 00:08:31.720
Now, law school tuition
has gone up even more,

00:08:31.720 --> 00:08:35.300
so I really feel for all
the current law students

00:08:35.300 --> 00:08:37.890
that are out there who are
racking up law school debt.

00:08:37.890 --> 00:08:38.730
It's crazy.

00:08:38.730 --> 00:08:41.450
It's everyone's personal horror story.

00:08:41.450 --> 00:08:43.890
It took me years to pay it off

00:08:43.890 --> 00:08:45.730
and it really impacted my quality of life.

00:08:45.730 --> 00:08:47.770
I don't regret going to law school,

00:08:47.770 --> 00:08:49.930
but all of the debt that you accumulate

00:08:49.930 --> 00:08:54.710
really does build up and, man, it's tough.

00:08:54.710 --> 00:08:57.790
So those were just a few
of the horror stories

00:08:57.790 --> 00:09:01.080
that I personally
experienced in law school.

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:03.070
If you want to hear more
stories about law school,

00:09:03.070 --> 00:09:05.740
check out this short
playlist that I put together

00:09:05.740 --> 00:09:08.350
that includes my personal
experiences with law school

00:09:08.350 --> 00:09:12.360
and, even better, strategies
on how to beat law school

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:15.710
once you get in and how
to get As in law school.

00:09:15.710 --> 00:09:17.850
Also, please subscribe,
really appreciate it,

00:09:17.850 --> 00:09:19.633
and I'll see you in the next video.

